Couldn't make it to CoRL this year, but I've been following the papers and discussions coming out of it.

A few things really stood out: how much compute we actually need, how we measure progress in robotics, and the growing gap between academic labs and industry.

Here's my roundup of CoRL and everything else that happened in robotics this week.

CoRL & Research

CoRL got 2,094 submissions this year, nearly 3x last year

SuperMimic from UC Berkeley won Best Paper. It trained a humanoid controller on 1 GPU in 3 hours, vs a baseline that needed 128 GPUs for 7 days (author's numbers)

A CoRL workshop paper called "How Much of Your VLA Is kNN" got 91.7% on the LIBERO benchmark without training a policy, which raises real questions about what our evals measure

In Hang Zhao's keynote, a policy calling a frontier model dropped from over 60% to 15% in a simulated kitchen once its thinking time counted against a deadline

Andy Pinxin Liu's post saying academic robotics has 2-3 years left got a lot of people talking

Models

@nvidia introduced GR00T 2, a world action model that learns from human video and carries skills across different robot bodies

@GoogleDeepMind released Gemini Robotics 2.5, and @Apptronik's Apollo 2 hit 96% success sorting car parts in a proof of concept on Mercedes-Benz production lines

@runwayml released open weights for Praxis-1, its 27B world action model, under Apache 2.0

OpenAI folded its stand-alone robotics team into its main research and applied teams, betting its general models can run robots

@SkildAI open-sourced Steel, a physics solver built with NVIDIA that runs up to 13x faster than PhysX on mixed robot tasks

Funding

Agile Robots raised a $500M Series F at an $8.5B valuation, led by SoftBank

@PathRobotics raised a $300M Series E at a $2.45B valuation to take its AI welding robots to more factory tasks

Deployments & Industry

@GXOLogistics + @Apptronik signed a letter of intent for up to 1,000 Apollo humanoids over four years, if trials hit their targets

@mimicrobotics + @BMW trained FLUX-mimic on real production parts for assembly, insertion, kitting and cable routing

@ASUS is training Gallop, its first humanoid, to pack motherboard accessories in its own factory

EACON's autonomous haulage system is now running on more than 4,000 mining trucks

Andy Rubin's Genki showed its humanoid in public for the first time

Japan's PM Sanae Takaichi met six Physical AI companies and restated her government's goal of around 10M AI robots by 2040

Really interesting week. I think the next few months will show which of these hold up in real deployments.

